<%
  /*
    Deeper dive: the closing invitation at the end of every Scott portal
    screen (14/09/2026, Tom's direction).

    Why it exists: somebody who has spent ten minutes inside a fictional
    company had no obvious way back to the real one. The portal ended
    wherever they happened to stop reading. This is that next step.

    Three things it deliberately is not:

    1. It is NOT a Scott offer. Scott is a demonstration of how the work
       is done, never a product with its own sales process. The CTA goes
       straight to Arrington's ordinary contact block on the main site
       (/#conversation, the same global footer every public page uses),
       so there is one enquiry route and no second funnel to maintain.
    2. It is NOT gated, counted, tracked or personalised. It is a link.
    3. It reads no record and takes no props, so it cannot leak anything.
       Every other block on these pages is clearance-filtered; this one
       has nothing to filter, which is what keeps it outside the access
       model entirely rather than being one more thing that has to be
       checked against a persona.

    Rendered immediately before </main> on every portal screen rather
    than on the dashboard alone, because "the end of the experience" is
    wherever the visitor stops, not where the site thinks they should.
    Excluded from login and the 404 page on purpose: neither is a place
    somebody has explored from.
  */
%>
<section class="sc-deeper" aria-labelledby="sc-deeper-heading">
  <div class="sc-deeper-words">
    <h2 class="sc-deeper-heading" id="sc-deeper-heading">Want to go deeper into Scott&rsquo;s Armchair?</h2>
    <p>You&rsquo;ve only scratched the surface. Scott&rsquo;s Armchair has customers, staff, vans, suppliers, money coming in and problems that don&rsquo;t always wait politely for Scott.</p>
    <p>If you&rsquo;d like a deeper dive into Scott&rsquo;s world, the armchairs, the knitting and the business behind it, get in touch.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="sc-deeper-cta">
    <%# The last word and the arrow are held together: at 390px the label
        wraps, and an arrow alone on its own line reads as a broken button
        rather than as a direction. %>
    <a href="/#conversation">Have a deeper dive into Scott&rsquo;s <span class="sc-deeper-nowrap">Armchair <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span></a>
  </p>
</section>
